  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and created the alert rule, and both exist in Kibana. The rule has run once with no error (status `ok`). I didn't check that its query actually matches the 5xx entries in the data.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view covers web server access logs, timestamped by `timestamp`. Each entry has the client IP (`clientip`), the requested URL (`url`, `request`), the HTTP status code (`response`, stored as text), response size (`bytes`), source and destination country (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), the browser (`agent`), the client's operating system (`machine.os`), file extension, `referer` and tags.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-311b9e6a Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-311b9e6a-web-logs-overview-b8b474ddc1c5`)\nIt has 7 panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single number.\n- **Unique visitors:** count of distinct client IPs.\n- **Requests over time:** line chart.\n- **Responses by status code:** pie chart of `response.keyword`.\n- **Bytes transferred over time:** area chart of total `bytes`.\n- **Top requested URLs:** table of the top 10 URLs with request count and average bytes.\n- **Top source countries:** bar chart of the top 10 by `geo.src`.\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-311b9e6a 5xx spike\"** (id `c71afe95-1cce-4a2d-8856-16273a74c34b`)\n- **Type:** Elasticsearch query, on `kibana_sample_data_logs` using the `timestamp` field.\n- **What it counts:** requests whose status code starts with \"5\" (a prefix match on `response.keyword`).\n- **Fires when:** the count is more than 10 within a 5-minute window.\n- **Schedule:** every 1 minute.\n- **State:** I created it switched on, so it is already running. Leave it on or turn it off as you prefer.\n- **Notifications:** none. It has no connector attached, so it will record alerts in Kibana but won't send anything. If you want notifications, a connector such as Slack or email needs to be added.",
